<details class="frame-walkthrough" data-frame-walkthrough>
  <summary
    ><span
      ><span class="eyebrow">LOOK A LITTLE DEEPER</span><strong
        >What happens during a frame?</strong
      ></span
    ><span class="optional-pill">Optional</span></summary
  >
  <div class="walk-content">
    <p>
      Think of a flipbook: each page shows the next moment. A game frame is one
      chance to handle input, move the game forward, and show the result. Unlike
      a flipbook, a game also listens to the player.
    </p>
    <p class="model-note">
      Browser learning model, not the running engine. This example shows a frame
      with one physics step due.
    </p>
    <ol class="frame-steps" data-frame-steps>
      <li>
        <span>1</span><strong>Prepare</strong><small
          >Make room for the next frame.</small>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>2</span><strong>Handle input</strong><small
          >Read the player’s actions.</small>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>3</span><strong>Advance physics</strong><small
          >Run a fixed-time tick when due.</small>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>4</span><strong>Update the world</strong><small
          >Apply this frame’s rules.</small>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>5</span><strong>Arrange the UI</strong><small
          >Update labels and layout.</small>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>6</span><strong>Present</strong><small
          >Show the finished output.</small>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="walk-controls">
      <button data-frame-next>Next step</button><button data-frame-reset
        >Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p data-frame-description role="status">
      The host supplies elapsed time. The engine coordinates the frame. Press
      “Next step” to follow it.
    </p>
    <a href="/manual/engine-details/runtime/">Read the exact runtime order</a>
  </div>
</details>
<script>
  document
    .querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-frame-walkthrough]")
    .forEach((root) => {
      let step = -1;
      const items = [
        ...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-frame-steps] li"),
      ];
      const next = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-frame-next]")!;
      const description = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
        "[data-frame-description]",
      )!;
      const descriptions = [
        "Prepare: open the native output batch and report changed dimensions before processing host input.",
        "Handle input: terminal input prepares the queue and routes focused UI and command events. Gameplay input reaches nodes during scene processing.",
        "Advance physics: run the fixed-time tick due in this example. A real frame can have zero or several fixed ticks.",
        "Update the world: run application and manager callbacks, then scene systems and nodes according to their phases.",
        "Arrange the UI: process pending reactive UI work, arrange elements, and draw into the pending output.",
        "Present: the host shows the completed output. A failed frame is aborted, preserving the original error.",
      ];
      const render = () => {
        items.forEach((item, i) => {
          item.dataset.active = String(i === step);
          item.dataset.complete = String(i < step);
        });
        description.textContent =
          step < 0
            ? "The host supplies elapsed time. The engine coordinates the frame. Press “Next step” to follow it."
            : descriptions[step];
        next.disabled = step === items.length - 1;
      };
      next.addEventListener("click", () => {
        if (step < items.length - 1) {
          step++;
          render();
        }
      });
      root
        .querySelector("[data-frame-reset]")!
        .addEventListener("click", () => {
          step = -1;
          render();
        });
      render();
    });
</script>
